steps.body:
Revaluing four desks is four independent jobs. With a plain `std::thread`, each job needs a variable to write its result into and a `join` before anyone reads it. `std::async` from `<future>` runs a function and hands back a `std::future<T>`, a handle to a value that isn't ready yet:

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

double desk_pnl(const std::vector<double>& book) {
    return std::accumulate(book.begin(), book.end(), 0.0);
}

int main() {
    const std::vector<double> book = {297.5, -5.0, 100.0};
    {{code}}
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
std::future<double> pnl = std::async(std::launch::async, desk_pnl, std::cref(book));
// ... other work while desk_pnl runs on its own thread ...
std::cout << pnl.get() << '\n';   // 392.5, once desk_pnl is done
```

- `get()` waits for the result and returns it. If the function threw, `get()` rethrows that exception in your thread, so a worker's error isn't lost. It works once: afterwards `valid()` is false.
- `wait()` blocks until the result is ready without taking it. Wait on every future before printing, and no task is still running while `main` writes its report.
- `std::async` copies its arguments the way `std::thread` does, so pass a big book with `std::cref`, the `const` form of the `std::ref` from the threads lesson.
- `std::launch::async` starts a new thread. `std::launch::deferred` runs nothing until `get()`, then runs the function on the calling thread. With no policy the library may pick either. libstdc++ starts a thread, but say `std::launch::async` whenever you need one.

One trap: the future from `std::async` waits for its task in its destructor. Drop it, and the call blocks until the job is done, so it is no way to fire and forget:

```cpp
std::atomic<bool> done{false};
std::async(std::launch::async, [&done] {
    std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(20));
    done = true;
});
std::cout << std::boolalpha << done << '\n';   // true: the dropped future waited
```

Each `std::async` here starts and joins a thread, which took about 100 to 200 microseconds in the sandbox (emulated on an Apple Silicon Mac, so rough), so it suits jobs that take milliseconds.

example.code:
#include <functional>
#include <future>
#include <iomanip>
#include <iostream>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <thread>
#include <vector>

struct Position {
    long long qty;
    double cost;
    double mark;
};

double desk_pnl(const std::vector<Position>& book) {
    double pnl = 0.0;
    for (const Position& p : book) {
        if (p.mark <= 0.0) {
            throw std::invalid_argument("no mark for a position");
        }
        pnl += p.qty * (p.mark - p.cost);
    }
    return pnl;
}

int main() {
    const std::vector<std::vector<Position>> desks = {
        {{100, 192.50, 193.10}, {-50, 410.00, 405.25}},
        {{300, 27.10, 27.45}, {200, 88.00, 87.20}, {-100, 15.50, 15.00}},
        {{10, 5000.00, 0.0}},  // the mark is missing
        {{1000, 9.95, 10.05}},
    };
    std::vector<std::future<double>> results;
    for (const auto& book : desks) {
        results.push_back(std::async(std::launch::async, desk_pnl, std::cref(book)));
    }
    for (std::future<double>& r : results) {
        r.wait();  // every desk is done before anything prints
    }

    std::cout << std::fixed << std::setprecision(2);
    double total = 0.0;
    for (std::size_t d = 0; d < results.size(); ++d) {
        try {
            const double pnl = results[d].get();  // desk d's value, or its exception rethrown
            std::cout << "desk " << d << ": " << pnl << '\n';
            total += pnl;
        } catch (const std::invalid_argument& e) {
            std::cout << "desk " << d << ": " << e.what() << '\n';
        }
    }
    std::cout << "total: " << total << '\n';
    std::cout << std::boolalpha << "future still valid: " << results[0].valid() << '\n';

    const std::thread::id main_id = std::this_thread::get_id();
    std::future<std::thread::id> lazy = std::async(std::launch::deferred, [] { return std::this_thread::get_id(); });
    std::cout << "deferred task ran on main: " << (lazy.get() == main_id) << '\n';
    return 0;
}

