steps.body:
A scenario grid for a risk report is hundreds of small jobs, and starting a thread for each costs more than the job. A **thread pool** starts a fixed number of threads once, and they take jobs from a queue until the pool shuts down. Its queue is the blocking queue from the threads lesson with jobs in place of ticks: a `std::mutex`, a `std::condition_variable` called `ready_`, a `std::queue`, and a `closed_` flag.

- Each worker waits on `ready_` for a job or for `closed_`, pops one job, and runs it with the lock released, so the other workers can take jobs meanwhile.
- `submit(f)` wraps `f` in a `std::packaged_task`, takes its future to return, and queues a small job that calls the task. `std::function` only holds callables it can copy, and a packaged task can't be copied, so the job holds a `std::shared_ptr` to it. C++23's `std::move_only_function` removes that step.
- The destructor sets `closed_`, wakes every worker with `notify_all()`, and joins them once they drain the queue.

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

class ThreadPool {
public:
    explicit ThreadPool(int threads) {
        for (int i = 0; i < threads; ++i) {
            workers_.emplace_back([this] { run(); });
        }
    }
    ~ThreadPool() {  // runs the jobs still queued, then joins
        {
            std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(m_);
            closed_ = true;
        }
        ready_.notify_all();
        for (std::thread& t : workers_) {
            t.join();
        }
    }
    {{code}}
private:
    void run() {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(m_);
        for (;;) {
            ready_.wait(lock, [this] { return !jobs_.empty() || closed_; });
            if (jobs_.empty()) return;  // closed and drained
            std::function<void()> job = std::move(jobs_.front());
            jobs_.pop();
            lock.unlock();
            job();  // outside the lock, so the other workers keep going
            lock.lock();
        }
    }
    std::mutex m_;
    std::condition_variable ready_;
    std::queue<std::function<void()>> jobs_;
    bool closed_ = false;
    std::vector<std::thread> workers_;
};

int main() {
    std::future<double> mid;
    {
        ThreadPool pool(2);
        mid = pool.submit([] { return (192.74 + 192.76) / 2; });
    }
    std::cout << mid.get() << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
template <typename F>
auto submit(F f) -> std::future<decltype(f())> {
    auto task = std::make_shared<std::packaged_task<decltype(f())()>>(std::move(f));
    auto result = task->get_future();
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(m_);
    jobs_.push([task] { (*task)(); });
    ready_.notify_one();
    return result;
}
```

`auto submit(F f) -> ...` is a trailing return type: it names the return type after the parameters, where `f` is in scope. `decltype(f())` is the type `f` returns, so a job that returns a `double` gives back a `std::future<double>`. Never let a job wait on another job's future in the same pool: when every worker waits, nobody is left to run what they wait for. To check a pool for data races, run it under the ThreadSanitizer from the threads lesson: on a Mac, build it with `clang++ -std=c++17 -g -O1 -fsanitize=thread` (the same flag works with g++ on Linux, and MSVC has no equivalent).

